<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<!--
  activeCodePage=UTF-8 makes the process ANSI code page UTF-8 (Windows 10 1903
  and later). Every ...A API then takes UTF-8, which is what lets a file name
  hold characters CP932 has no room for: open(), stat() and friends are all
  reached through fileconvsto(), and with 'jmask' asking for UTF-8 as the system
  code the name now goes to them unchanged.

  longPathAware lifts the 260 character limit on a path. It needs Windows 10 1607
  or later with LongPathsEnabled in
  HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\FileSystem, which is off by default, so
  this changes nothing on a machine that has not turned it on. Where it is on,
  the ...A file APIs (open, stat, CreateFile) start accepting the long ones;
  GetFullPathName does not, whatever the manifest says, which is why FullName()
  in winjnt.c goes through the wide call instead.

  Note for the next person editing this file: it is XML, so a comment may not
  contain a double hyphen. One here made the manifest invalid, and Windows then
  refused to start the exe at all with a side-by-side configuration error.

  dpiAware/dpiAwareness say the process places its own pixels. Without them
  Windows draws the whole window at 96 DPI and then stretches the result to the
  display scale, which resamples finished glyphs. Text comes out soft and
  uneven, in the menu bar as much as in the text, and even a BDF font, which GDI
  never antialiases, arrives blurred. Being DPI aware costs nothing here: the
  character grid does not need to reflow, only the font has to be asked for in
  real pixels, which winjnt.c does (see dpi_scale_to()).

  Deliberately absent:
    - comctl32 v6: that would swap the dialogs for themed ones.
    - supportedOS: it would change what GetVersionEx() reports, which parts of
      winjnt.c still branch on.
-->
<assembly xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1" manifestVersion="1.0">
  <assemblyIdentity
      type="win32"
      name="JVim.JVim"
      version="3.0.2.1"
      processorArchitecture="*"/>
  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <activeCodePage xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2019/WindowsSettings">UTF-8</activeCodePage>
      <longPathAware xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">true</longPathAware>
      <!--
        Per monitor from Windows 10 1703, per monitor on 8.1, system DPI before
        that. Windows reads the first level in the list it understands, so the
        older dpiAware element is only there for the systems that have never
        heard of dpiAwareness.
      -->
      <dpiAwareness xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">PerMonitorV2, PerMonitor, System</dpiAwareness>
      <dpiAware xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2005/WindowsSettings">true/pm</dpiAware>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>
</assembly>
